---
/**
 * Install — the install one-liner, the two alternatives, and the "what next"
 * verification line.
 *
 * Copy is rendered verbatim from docs/copy.md §Install (ADR-0003: the copy
 * deck is the single source of truth). Every string sits on ONE source line
 * and is never split by an inline element, so the built output greps literally
 * against the deck (convention from phase-1-ws-1.1, documented in 1.2 and
 * 1.3) — with one forced exception:
 *
 *   - The alternatives line contains a markdown link, `[release](…)`. That is
 *     a functional link, not typography, so it has to be a real <a> and the
 *     sentence cannot be one text node under any implementation. It splits at
 *     the link and nowhere else: the text before it, the label, and the
 *     trailing "." are each still byte-for-byte deck text. The period stays
 *     OUTSIDE the anchor because that is where the deck puts it.
 *   - The backticked commands (`go install …`, `metis init`) stay plain text.
 *     Wrapping them in <code> would split two more strings for typography
 *     alone — the trade phase-1-ws-1.3 refused for `metis next`. It also keeps
 *     the two adjacent lines consistent: the only mono treatment here is the
 *     <pre> block, which the deck asks for explicitly.
 *
 * The one-liner is deliberately the same string the Hero renders: the deck
 * publishes it twice (§Hero "beneath the terminal", §Install "mono block"),
 * so two occurrences in dist is the specified state, not a duplication bug.
 *
 * Every claim here was checked against the real CLI and the real repository
 * before it was written (OVERVIEW.md §3.4): scripts/install.sh and
 * cmd/metis/main.go both resolve on main, go.mod declares the module path the
 * `go install` line uses, and `metis init` exists in v0.0.6 with no required
 * flags and does print its next steps.
 *
 * This section is the last child of <main> — the footer is a body-level
 * sibling now (ADR-0004) — so the shared `.section` hairline below it reads as
 * the rule that separates page content from the footer.
 *
 * Reveal (ADR-0005): the section opts into the page's shared observer with
 * `data-reveal`, and its four blocks carry `.reveal` so they arrive in the
 * order they are meant to be executed in — headline, the command you run,
 * the alternatives if that one does not suit, then what to do next. Nothing
 * sets a hidden base state: the rules below render the finished section and
 * the reveal is opt-in motion layered on top (the never-invisible rule).
 * That matters more here than anywhere else on the page — this section is
 * the install command, and it has to be readable and copyable for a visitor
 * with JS disabled.
 */
---

<section id="install" class="section" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="reveal">Two minutes to a governed project.</h2>

  <pre
    class="one-liner reveal"><code>curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/techspeque/metis/main/scripts/install.sh | bash</code></pre>

  <p class="alternatives reveal">or go install github.com/techspeque/metis/cmd/metis@latest, or grab a <a
      href="https://github.com/techspeque/metis/releases"
      rel="noopener">release</a>.</p>

  <p class="verify reveal">then: metis init — it prints every next step.</p>
</section>

<style>
  .section {
    padding-block: var(--space-8);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  /* Matches the Hero's install block: the global `pre` rule already supplies
     the surface, hairline and padding, so this only sets the rhythm above it
     and steps the size down one token — a shell command is reference material,
     not body copy. */
  .one-liner {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  /* Muted on base: 8.39:1 dark, 5.66:1 light (tokens.css ratio table). The
     "release" link inside inherits the global anchor colour — accent on base,
     8.17:1 dark / 5.53:1 light — so both AA pairings are already documented. */
  .alternatives,
  .verify {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  /* The verification line is the section's payoff — the one instruction the
     reader leaves with — so it carries full-strength text (17.68:1 in both
     schemes) and a wider gap above it than the alternatives line. */
  .verify {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-4);
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  /* --- Reveal stagger ----------------------------------------------------
     Choreography only: index.astro owns the animation, this component owns
     the order. The delays are inert custom properties until that rule reads
     them, which happens only once `is-revealed` lands and only outside
     reduced motion — so no media query is needed here.

     80ms apart, in execution order, the h2 taking the 0ms default. The
     one-liner is second on purpose: it is the thing the visitor came for,
     so it should not be waiting behind the two lines that qualify it. */
  .one-liner {
    --reveal-delay: 80ms;
  }

  .alternatives {
    --reveal-delay: 160ms;
  }

  .verify {
    --reveal-delay: 240ms;
  }
</style>
